  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Phellinus lundellii, commonly known as birch bristle bracket, is a woody fungus that forms hard, shelf-like fruiting bodies on birch trees throughout New England. As a member of the Hymenochaetaceae family, it is a wood-decay fungus that plays an important ecological role in decomposing dead birch wood.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Host Preference",
      "description": "Nearly exclusive to birch trees (Betula species), making host identification a strong clue",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bristle-like Pores",
      "description": "The underside features distinctive small, sharp, bristle-like pores that are characteristic of the species and give it its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Hard, Woody Bracket",
      "description": "Forms hard, shelf-like fruiting bodies that are difficult to break and persist on wood for years",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Phellinus lundellii grows on dead or dying birch trees and woody debris in mixed forests and woodlands throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many bracket fungi, the bristle-like pores of P. lundellii are so sharp that they can feel almost hair-like to the touch, distinguishing it from smoother-pored relatives.",
